    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Chat.Text" xml:space="preserve">The chat list is on the left: conversations can be pinned, renamed and deleted. A message can carry attached files and images. Up to three turns run at once in different chats - while the model answers in one, you can work in another.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Chat.Title" xml:space="preserve">Conversations and attachments</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Confirm.Text" xml:space="preserve">Deleting things, writing to the registry, installing software and anything else irreversible is asked about separately and waits for your answer. The prompt says in plain words what is about to happen, and “Technical details” holds the exact command or script. Refusing is a normal reply: the model sees it and looks for another way. There is a mode that confirms everything automatically, but that is a deliberate choice, not the default.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Confirm.Title" xml:space="preserve">Confirming dangerous actions</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Cost.Text" xml:space="preserve">Above every answer you can see what it cost, and next to the input box are what is left on the Venice balance and how full the context is. The badge changes colour as the money runs low, so the credits will not quietly run out mid-task.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Cost.Title" xml:space="preserve">Cost and balance</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Data.Text" xml:space="preserve">Conversations, settings and attachments live in the profile folder on this computer and go nowhere else. The neighbouring Data Controls page shows how much space they take; the same page exports everything as a single archive and restores it back.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Data.Title" xml:space="preserve">Where the data lives</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Models.Text" xml:space="preserve">The model is chosen in the row under the input box, and so is the reasoning strength. “Auto” picks the model for the task itself: a simple question goes to a cheap one, heavy analysis to a strong one. The service models behind chat titles and the agent's internal tasks are configured separately, on the Customize page.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Models.Title" xml:space="preserve">Model and reasoning level</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Profiles.Text" xml:space="preserve">Profiles keep conversations and settings apart for several people sharing one computer, and a profile can be locked with a password. The Appearance page holds themes, a custom accent colour, the backdrop, the glass effect and the interface scale.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Profiles.Title" xml:space="preserve">Profiles and appearance</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Section" xml:space="preserve">Using the app</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Tools.Text" xml:space="preserve">The model does more than answer: it reads and writes files, runs PowerShell, inspects Windows event logs and searches the web. Every tool call is reported inside the conversation - expand it to see exactly what was done.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Basics.Tools.Title" xml:space="preserve">Tools</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Intro" xml:space="preserve">The app talks to Venice.ai models and administers this computer on their instructions. It needs a Venice key to work - below are five steps from signing up to the first answer.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Links.Docs" xml:space="preserve">Venice documentation</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Links.Repo" xml:space="preserve">Project repository</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Links.Text" xml:space="preserve">The Venice documentation answers questions about keys, limits and models. Questions about the app itself, bugs you find and suggestions belong in the project repository.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Links.Title" xml:space="preserve">If something does not add up</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Chat" xml:space="preserve">Do not paste the key into the chat: the message goes to the model and then stays in the conversation history on disk.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Config" xml:space="preserve">Do not put the key into appsettings.json next to the program: it is a plain text file that anyone who reaches the folder can read. Keys from the Key &amp; Info page live elsewhere and are encrypted.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Crash" xml:space="preserve">Crash reports have the key stripped out on their own, so a report is safe to forward.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Revoke" xml:space="preserve">If the key does leak, revoke it on venice.ai and create a new one - that is a single button.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Scope" xml:space="preserve">Create the variable for the current user, not for the whole system: a system variable is visible to every account on the computer.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="S.Info.Safety.Title" xml:space="preserve">Treat the key like a password</system:String>
